# packages/anchor — environment
#
# Copy to .env and fill in. .env is gitignored; no secrets in the repo.
#   cp packages/anchor/.env.example packages/anchor/.env
#   pnpm --filter @nns/anchor build
#   pnpm --filter @nns/anchor deploy          # dry run, sends nothing
#   pnpm --filter @nns/anchor deploy --send   # broadcasts
#   pnpm --filter @nns/anchor verify 0x…      # does that address hold this contract?
#
# Deploy to Sepolia first. It rehearses this exact path — same bytecode, same
# script, same variables — and what it rehearses is the configuration, which
# is where the mistakes are.

# ── Endpoint (required, no defaults) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing here is defaulted on purpose. A defaulted chain id would agree with
# a stale RPC URL, and deploying to the wrong network succeeds silently.
#
# Polygon PoS mainnet: 137        Sepolia: 11155111
NNS_ANCHOR_RPC_URL=
NNS_ANCHOR_CHAIN_ID=

# ── Deploy key (one of the two) ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# The key signs in-process — unlike the Nimiq admin key, there is no node
# wallet to keep it in — so it comes from the environment and nowhere else.
# It is needed only to send.
#
# For a dry run, set the ADDRESS instead and leave the KEY blank: the plan
# reads a nonce and a balance and predicts the CREATE address, none of which
# need a key. That is the point of a cold deploy key — the plan can be built
# and reviewed on a machine that has never held it.
#
# If both are set they must agree; the script refuses otherwise.
NNS_ANCHOR_DEPLOY_KEY=
NNS_ANCHOR_DEPLOY_ADDRESS=

# ── Publisher (cron: pnpm --filter @nns/anchor publisher [--send]) ───────────
# Dry run by default; --send pins the log snapshot and broadcasts the anchor.
# Shares NNS_ANCHOR_RPC_URL / NNS_ANCHOR_CHAIN_ID above.
#
# §9's cadence is on change with a daily floor: the run anchors when the
# commitment moved since this key's last anchor, unconditionally when that
# anchor is a day old, and otherwise exits clean saying "unchanged". So the
# schedule below is how often the publisher *looks*, not how often it
# anchors — every few hours is right:
#   17 */4 * * *  cd …/nns && pnpm --filter @nns/anchor publisher --send
# Exit 1 is an alert; wire it to whatever pages you.

# Where NnsAnchor lives — an output of the deploy script, verified against
# the committed artifact on every run before anything is sent.
NNS_ANCHOR_CONTRACT_ADDRESS=

# The NNS API serving /log and /checkpoints/{height}.
NNS_ANCHOR_API_URL=

# Implementation A: a kubo RPC add endpoint (POST {url}/api/v0/add) —
# typically the operator's own kubo node. AUTH, if set, is sent verbatim as
# the Authorization header.
NNS_ANCHOR_IPFS_ADD_URL=
NNS_ANCHOR_IPFS_ADD_AUTH=

# Implementation B — §8.2 requires a genuinely *independent second
# implementation*, and the snapshot is anchored only when both mint the same
# CID. Configure exactly one of the two below; both set is refused.
#
# Named default: Filebase (free tier), via its S3-compatible API. It runs
# its own importer and reports the minted CID in the x-amz-meta-cid response
# header; the publisher's agreement gate is what verifies its parameters
# match §8.2 on every run. Bucket must be an IPFS-backed Filebase bucket.
NNS_ANCHOR_FILEBASE_BUCKET=
NNS_ANCHOR_FILEBASE_KEY=
NNS_ANCHOR_FILEBASE_SECRET=
# Optional; defaults to https://s3.filebase.com
NNS_ANCHOR_FILEBASE_ENDPOINT=
#
# Alternative: any second kubo-RPC-shaped endpoint that is NOT the same
# implementation as A. The publisher refuses identical URLs, but only you
# can make the implementations independent.
NNS_ANCHOR_IPFS_ADD_URL_2=
NNS_ANCHOR_IPFS_ADD_AUTH_2=

# The publisher key — the repo's one hot EVM key (SHOULD be a multisig
# signer, §9). Needed only to send; for a dry run set the ADDRESS instead
# and the whole plan runs on a machine that has never held the key.
NNS_ANCHOR_PUBLISHER_KEY=
NNS_ANCHOR_PUBLISHER_ADDRESS=

# §11.5 alert threshold, wei. The publisher refuses to sign when the balance
# cannot cover one anchor; below this threshold it *warns* while still
# anchoring — size it so topping up is routine, not an incident.
# 0.1 POL = 100000000000000000.
NNS_ANCHOR_MIN_BALANCE_WEI=

# Optional: how many anchor-chain blocks back eth_getLogs looks for this
# publisher's own anchors (idempotency + gap reporting). Default 100000,
# ~2.3 days on Polygon PoS. Public endpoints with tight getLogs range caps
# may need it lowered.
NNS_ANCHOR_LOOKBACK_BLOCKS=

# ── Not here, and deliberately ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# The chain id and contract address are configuration, not protocol
# constants (§9), and must not be added to @nns/core. ANCHOR_PUBLISHERS is
# the *reader's* client-side list and no business of the publisher.
